SUMMARY:Elevator to the Gallows w/Q&A | Additional Films and Guests
DESCRIPTION:For his feature debut\, 24-year-old Louis Malle brought toget
 her a mesmerizing performance by Jeanne Moreau\, evocative cinematography
  by Henri Decaë\, and a now legendary jazz score by Miles Davis. Taking 
 place over the course of one restless Paris night\, Malle's richly atmosp
 heric crime thriller stars Moreau and Maurice Ronet as lovers whose plan 
 to murder her husband (his boss) goes awry\, setting off a chain of event
 s that seals their fate. A career touchstone for its director and female 
 star\, Elevator to the Gallows was an astonishing beginning to Malle's ec
 lectic body of work\, and it established Moreau as one of the most captiv
 ating actors ever to grace the screen. [91 min\; drama\, film noir\; Fren
 ch with English subtitles]A Q&amp\;A with David Brent Johnson (WFIU\, pro
 ducer and jazz director) and James Naremore (IU professor emeritus\, film
  noir scholar) will follow the screening.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  For his feature debut\, 24-year-old Lou
 is Malle brought together a mesmerizing performance by Jeanne Moreau\, ev
 ocative cinematography by Henri Decaë\, and a now legendary jazz score b
 y Miles Davis. Taking place over the course of one restless Paris night\,
  Malle's richly atmospheric crime thriller stars Moreau and Maurice Ronet
  as lovers whose plan to murder her husband (his boss) goes awry\, settin
 g off a chain of events that seals their fate. A career touchstone for it
 s director and female star\, <i>Elevator to the Gallows</i> was an astoni
 shing beginning to Malle's eclectic body of work\, and it established Mor
 eau as one of the most captivating actors ever to grace the screen. [91 m
 in\; drama\, film noir\; French with English subtitles]</p><p><i><strong>
 A Q&amp\;A with David Brent Johnson (WFIU\, producer and jazz director) a
 nd James Naremore (IU professor emeritus\, film noir scholar) will follow
  the screening.</strong></i></p>
